            "count": 0,
            "text": "utils::available.packages() is called repeatedly, within the lifetime of its repository cache, by install.packages(), old.packages(), update.packages(), packageStatus() and many package-management tools. Profiling a cached call against CRAN (25,299 source packages) on R-devel shows that nearly all of its ~140 ms goes into two places: readRDS() of the cached PACKAGES.rds, which CRAN and Bioconductor serve xz-compressed (~90 ms), and the default \"R_version\" filter (~43 ms). With overlapping repositories, e.g. CRAN together with a dated Posit Package Manager snapshot, the default \"duplicates\" filter and the opt-in \"CRAN\" filter are also quadratic in the number of duplicated packages and take seconds. On Windows, the \"subarch\" filter adds ~35 ms per call.\n\nBelow are five independent patches, one per hot spot, attached separately. None of them changes results: in every measurement below the patched and unpatched results were identical(), and each new regression test also passes on unpatched trunk.\n\nReproducible example (timings on aarch64-apple-darwin, R-devel r90607):\n\n    options(repos = c(CRAN = \"https://cloud.r-project.org\"))\n    invisible(available.packages())      # downloads and fills the cache\n    system.time(available.packages())    # ~140 ms, from the cache\n    system.time(readRDS(list.files(tempdir(), \"^repos_.*\\\\.rds$\", full.names = TRUE)[1]))\n    # ~90 ms: the xz-compressed index as served by CRAN\n\n    ## overlapping repositories at different versions\n    options(repos = c(CRAN = \"https://cloud.r-project.org\",\n                      PPM = \"https://packagemanager.posit.co/cran/2025-01-02\"))\n    db <- available.packages(filters = list())           # ~47,000 rows, ~20,000 duplicated\n    system.time(tools:::.remove_stale_dups(db))          # ~1.2 s (the \"duplicates\" filter)\n    system.time(utils:::available_packages_filters_db$CRAN(db))  # ~3.4 s (the \"CRAN\" filter)\n\nThe patches:\n\n1. available-packages-cache-zstd.diff (src/library/utils/R/packages.R, tests/internet2.R)\n\n   The cache stores PACKAGES.rds exactly as downloaded, so every cached call pays for xz decompression again. After a download, the cache is now rewritten from the index just read with zstdfile(dest, \"wb\", compression = 1L), or uncompressed in builds without zstd; the PACKAGES.gz / PACKAGES fallback goes through the same code instead of its own saveRDS(compress = TRUE). Level 1 writes about three times as fast as the default level and reads just as fast (CRAN source: 1.2 MB xz read in 90 ms vs 1.8 MB zstd read in 27 ms). Two related changes: a cache file that cannot be read (e.g. a zstd cache in a user cache dir read by a build without zstd, or a file another R process is still writing) is now treated as a miss and downloaded again rather than being an error, and an empty index is never left in the cache; need_dest is gone since the cache is always written when enabled. Warm available.packages() calls: 141 -> 82 ms for CRAN, 170 -> 102 ms for CRAN + 3 Bioconductor repos, 196 -> 92 ms for CRAN macOS binaries; cold calls (the one that downloads) cost 21-29 ms more for the rewrite. A test in tests/internet2.R checks the zstd magic number, that the cached call matches the downloading one, and that an unreadable cache is re-downloaded.\n\n2. available-packages-r-version-filter.diff (src/library/utils/R/packages.R, tests/reg-tests-1e.R)\n\n   The \"R_version\" filter strips and splits Depends for every row with a per-row closure, then parses every R dependency with numeric_version(). CRAN has ~20,800 R dependencies but only ~180 distinct ones. The patch skips rows whose Depends never matches \"R[[:space:]]*\\(\" up front, splits the rest in one vectorized pass recording the db row of each entry, compares each distinct R dependency once and matches the results back. The per-row all() reduction is unchanged, so NA semantics are the same (an NA-only comparison still errors, FALSE + NA is still dropped). 43 -> 17 ms for CRAN, 85 -> 35 ms for CRAN + PPM.\n\n3. available-packages-duplicates-filter.diff (src/library/tools/R/packages.R, tests/reg-tests-1e.R)\n\n   tools:::.remove_stale_dups(), which implements the \"duplicates\" filter and is also used by old.packages(), packageStatus(), write_PACKAGES(latestOnly = TRUE) and update_PACKAGES(), loops over the duplicated package names and for each runs which() over all rows and package_version() on that package's versions. Repeated (Package, Version) pairs are now always dropped first (as the > 100 duplicates branch did), and the remaining entries of duplicated packages are ordered all at once: by package (grouped with match(), so collation does not matter), then by decreasing version via xtfrm(package_version(unique versions)), then by row; all but the first per package are dropped. Ties keep the first row and rows keep their order and row names, as before. 1,164 -> 24 ms for CRAN + PPM, 17 -> 6 ms for r-universe + CRAN; with only a handful of duplicates it is about 1 ms slower (4 -> 5 ms), since the (Package, Version) pass now always runs.\n\n4. available-packages-cran-filter.diff (src/library/utils/R/packages.R, tests/reg-tests-1e.R)\n\n   The \"CRAN\" filter loops over duplicated names with which(packages == d) per name. One startsWith() over Repository and one %in% now find the non-CRAN entries of packages that also have a CRAN entry; the result is selected with a logical index, which keeps the same rows in the same order. 3,385 -> 3 ms for CRAN + PPM, 2,725 -> 2 ms for CRAN listed twice.\n\n5. available-packages-subarch-filter.diff (src/library/utils/R/packages.R, tests/reg-tests-1e.R)\n\n   The \"subarch\" filter (active only when .Platform$r_arch is non-empty, as on Windows) runs strsplit() in a per-row closure over the whole Archs column, although CRAN's Windows index has only two distinct non-NA values. The distinct entries are now split and checked once and matched back; NA rows are kept as before. 35 -> 2 ms for CRAN's Windows binaries (measured on macOS with .Platform$r_arch substituted by \"x64\" in both versions of the function). The regression test has explicit expectations for r_arch \"\", \"x64\", \"i386\" and other values, so it exercises the filter on Windows and returns early elsewhere.\n\nAll patches are against r90609 and apply with patch -p0, each on its own or all five together in any order (the tests are at different spots in reg-tests-1e.R). make test-Reg passes with each of them applied. Each is also submitted as a GitHub pull request with CI results and a few more measurements:\n\n  1. https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/351\n  2. https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/352\n  3. https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/353\n  4. https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/354\n  5. https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/355",
